{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791709692,"nonce":"LVgg18aI2bxOfiasTh2IsPC2EgU=","title":"A keyframed rotate(360deg) does not turn","message":"The wiki's Motion page has a spinner, `@keyframes k-spin { to { transform: rotate(360deg) } }`, that stands still.\n\nCause: blinc_abi parses a transform declaration straight to an Affine2D, and native motion blends transforms as matrices, decomposed and turned the short way round. rotate(360deg) is the identity matrix, the same as `none`, so the track goes from identity to identity and nothing moves. rotate(270deg) turns a quarter back instead of three quarters on.\n\nCSS interpolates two transform lists of the same shape function by function, by their arguments, and falls back to matrices only when the shapes differ.\n\nFix (native motion only, no blinc_abi change): keyframe stops arrive as declaration text, so `motion/functions.rs` reads a stop's transform into its functions (translate, scale, rotate, skew and their axis forms; `matrix()` and anything unread fall back). A track keeps each stop's function list beside its write. When both ends of a span have lists of one shape, or one end is `none`, which stands for the other's identities, the span blends by argument and composes. Otherwise it blends as before.\n\nNot covered: transitions. Their ends come from the cascade already parsed, so `transition: transform` from rotate(0) to rotate(270deg) still goes the short way. Doing that needs the declared text, or the function list, to reach motion from the cascade.\n\nTests:\n- `motion::tests::a_keyframed_rotate_turns_by_its_angle_not_the_short_way_between_matrices` checks 90° at a quarter and a 270° turn the long way.\n- `tests/native-motion.mjs` checks pixels: a bar keyframed to rotate(360deg) stands upright at a quarter and lies flat again at half.\n- Both fail with the function-list path disabled.","files":null}]}